  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A person whose mass is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> travels from Earth to Mars in a spaceship. Neglect all other objects in sky and take acceleration due to gravity on the<br />surface of the Earth and Mars as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>, respectively. Identify from the below figures, the curve that fits best for the weight of the passenger as a function of time.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0118_58694fdbc050.png\" style=\"width: 340px; height: 221px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>)</mo></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>)</mo></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When the passenger moves away from the Earth at some point, gravitational force due to earth will be minimised and then start increasing as the passenger moves towards&nbsp;Mars as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo></math>also gravitational force cannot be negative.&nbsp;Therefore, the correct curve that fits best for the weight of the passenger as a function of time is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mfenced></math>.</p>"
  }
